{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"4bfb4013739dc069","name":"card_event_strike_group","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/USA/NavalWarfare/events/card_event_strike_group","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"STRIKE GROUP","text":"Show when drawn: Draw a random air unit from your deck.\r\nYour air units get +1+1.","flavorText":"A carrier strike group is a type of battle group of the US Navy. During WWII, it was primarily referred to as a Fast Carrier Task Force, a powerful and flexible naval formation utilizing aircraft carriers to conduct offensive operations. Unlike traditional fleets built around battleships, these groups centered on fast aircraft carriers escorted by cruisers, destroyers, and submarines. This formation allowed for rapid, long-range strikes across the Pacific. Carrier groups such as Task Force 58 projected US air power deep into enemy territory. In campaigns such as the Gilberts, the Marshalls, and the Marianas, their aircraft neutralized airfields, bombed installations, and supported amphibious landings. At the Battle of the Philippine Sea in 1944, coordinated strikes from these groups overwhelmed Japanese airpower in what became known as the \"Great Marianas Turkey Shoot\". The mobility and concentration of air assets gave the US a decisive edge. Each carrier operated dozens of fighters, dive bombers, and torpedo aircraft. Their escorts screened against submarines and surface threats, while logistics ships kept them supplied at sea. These strike groups proved essential in turning the tide of the Pacific War. They bypassed strongpoints, isolated garrisons, and delivered sustained pressure on Japan’s shrinking perimeter.","faction":"USA","type":"order","rarity":"Uncommon","cost":3,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"GameplayTags":["ability.showwhendrawn"],"Name":"card_event_strike_group","title":{"Namespace":"card_usa","Key":"card_event_strike_group_title","SourceString":"STRIKE GROUP","LocalizedString":"STRIKE GROUP"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::USA","kredits":3,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_usa","Key":"card_event_strike_group_text","SourceString":"Show when drawn: Draw a random air unit from your deck.\r\nYour air units get +1+1.","LocalizedString":"Show when drawn: Draw a random air unit from your deck.\r\nYour air units get +1+1."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_usa","Key":"card_event_strike_group_history","SourceString":"A carrier strike group is a type of battle group of the US Navy. During WWII, it was primarily referred to as a Fast Carrier Task Force, a powerful and flexible naval formation utilizing aircraft carriers to conduct offensive operations. Unlike traditional fleets built around battleships, these groups centered on fast aircraft carriers escorted by cruisers, destroyers, and submarines. This formation allowed for rapid, long-range strikes across the Pacific. Carrier groups such as Task Force 58 projected US air power deep into enemy territory. In campaigns such as the Gilberts, the Marshalls, and the Marianas, their aircraft neutralized airfields, bombed installations, and supported amphibious landings. At the Battle of the Philippine Sea in 1944, coordinated strikes from these groups overwhelmed Japanese airpower in what became known as the \"Great Marianas Turkey Shoot\". The mobility and concentration of air assets gave the US a decisive edge. Each carrier operated dozens of fighters, dive bombers, and torpedo aircraft. Their escorts screened against submarines and surface threats, while logistics ships kept them supplied at sea. These strike groups proved essential in turning the tide of the Pacific War. They bypassed strongpoints, isolated garrisons, and delivered sustained pressure on Japan’s shrinking perimeter.","LocalizedString":"A carrier strike group is a type of battle group of the US Navy. During WWII, it was primarily referred to as a Fast Carrier Task Force, a powerful and flexible naval formation utilizing aircraft carriers to conduct offensive operations. Unlike traditional fleets built around battleships, these groups centered on fast aircraft carriers escorted by cruisers, destroyers, and submarines. This formation allowed for rapid, long-range strikes across the Pacific. Carrier groups such as Task Force 58 projected US air power deep into enemy territory. In campaigns such as the Gilberts, the Marshalls, and the Marianas, their aircraft neutralized airfields, bombed installations, and supported amphibious landings. At the Battle of the Philippine Sea in 1944, coordinated strikes from these groups overwhelmed Japanese airpower in what became known as the \"Great Marianas Turkey Shoot\". The mobility and concentration of air assets gave the US a decisive edge. Each carrier operated dozens of fighters, dive bombers, and torpedo aircraft. Their escorts screened against submarines and surface threats, while logistics ships kept them supplied at sea. These strike groups proved essential in turning the tide of the Pacific War. They bypassed strongpoints, isolated garrisons, and delivered sustained pressure on Japan’s shrinking perimeter."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Uncommon","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::NavalWarfare","draftBaseWeight":0.8,"soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'Naval_Support_17'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/Assets/Audio/Misc/OrdersFeb17/Naval_Support_17.0"},"Image":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/Images/USA/NavalWarfare/t_strike_group.t_strike_group","SubPathString":""},"artist":"Tom Freeman","historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/STRIKE_GROUP.STRIKE_GROUP","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoSize":{"X":512.0,"Y":382.0},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_usa","Key":"card_event_strike_group_photo","SourceString":"US Third Fleet aircraft carriers at anchor in Ulithi Atoll, December 1944.","LocalizedString":"US Third Fleet aircraft carriers at anchor in Ulithi Atoll, December 1944."},"OnAttackVisu